## 8. Voice and Audio

- Voice greeting on opening: name, date, time of day.
- Every reminder, game instruction, and "Read aloud" uses text-to-speech with a replay speaker button.
- Use the browser speech APIs behind a small wrapper so the voice layer can be turned off without changing screens.
- **Voice failure fallback:** on-screen buttons always remain. If audio is blocked, show a captions banner with the same text.
- Voice message language is chosen per reminder by the caregiver.
- Support for Bodo, Mizo, Khasi, Nagamese, and Manipuri speech varies by device. Show text in the chosen language even when voice falls back to a nearby language, and never show a technical error.

---

## 9. Motion and Feedback

- Transitions 200 to 300ms, ease-out. No bouncing, shaking, flashing, or fast-moving elements in the patient view.
- Tap feedback: soft scale pulse (about 3%) plus a gentle tone. Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` by removing motion and keeping the tone.
- Badge pulse for new alerts (professional views only), stops after 3 pulses.
- Never auto-advance a patient screen without a clear tap, except the result screen after a game.

---

## 10. Offline and Sync

| Situation | Patient sees | Caregiver / Healthcare Worker sees |
|---|---|---|
| Offline | Nothing changes | Chip: "Offline, changes saved on device" |
| Play while offline | Nothing changes | Tag on session: "Saved on device" |
| Acknowledge alert offline | n/a | Alert shows "Will sync" |
| Connection returns | Nothing changes | Chip changes to "Synced just now" |

Implementation for the demo: keep a local queue in memory or local storage, and provide a hidden toggle to simulate offline and online.

---

## 11. Accessibility Checklist

- Visible focus ring (3px, `--blue`) on every interactive element.
- Every icon button has an accessible label.
- Screen reader order matches visual order.
- Minimum contrast met in all themes.
- No timeouts that cause loss of progress. Mid-game exit always saves.
- No flashing content.
- Forms: inline plain-language messages, never color-only errors.

